Banking
15 themes · 30 use cases
Stopping scam payments before they leave
Scam warnings that fit the moment
The problem to solve
Generic warnings ('Are you sure?') are ignored, while blocking too many payments angers genuine customers. Build an AI model that scores each outgoing payment for scam risk using the payee, the customer's behaviour (for example, being on a long phone call while paying), device signals and known scam patterns, and shows a specific warning or pause that matches the likely type of scam.

Stopping invoice-redirection fraud against businesses
The problem to solve
Criminals hack or imitate a supplier's email and send 'updated bank details', so that a business pays its genuine invoices into a criminal account. In one widely reported case, an employee of engineering firm Arup in Hong Kong sent about $25 million after a deepfake video call. Build an AI tool that flags payments to new or recently changed beneficiaries that do not match the supplier's history and prompts a call-back check.

Digital identity you can trust
Catching deepfakes and injected video in selfie checks
The problem to solve
Fraudsters can now fool selfie and video checks with synthetic faces, face-swap apps, masks or fake camera feeds, and these attacks are rising quickly. Build an AI defence that detects presentation attacks (photos, screens, masks) and injection attacks (virtual cameras, emulators), looks for signs of manipulation in the video and the device, and explains each risk score to the fraud team.

Accepting the new digital identity wallets
The problem to solve
Governments are launching digital identity wallets that let people share verified details from their phone, but each scheme has different standards, data and trust levels. Build an AI-assisted onboarding service that accepts credentials from several digital ID schemes, checks they are valid, maps the data into the bank's systems and decides when extra checks are still needed.

Early warning for business borrowers
Warning signs in the borrower's own transactions
The problem to solve
A business's bank account shows stress long before its accounts do: falling sales receipts, late payments to suppliers or tax authorities, payroll cuts or growing reliance on the overdraft. Build an AI model that watches these patterns, scores each borrower's risk every week, explains what has changed and alerts the relationship manager with suggested actions.

When a borrower's key customer or supplier fails
The problem to solve
A healthy borrower can be dragged down when a major customer goes bust or a key supplier stops delivering, but banks rarely map these links. Build an AI tool that builds a map of borrowers' main customers and suppliers from payment flows and public data, watches for trouble among them and alerts the bank to borrowers who are exposed.

Fake applicants and fake documents
Spotting synthetic identities
The problem to solve
Synthetic identities are built up slowly over months, with small credit lines repaid on time, before the fraudster borrows heavily and disappears. Build an AI model that links applications, devices, addresses, phone numbers and credit histories to spot identities that look 'too new' or share hidden connections, and explains why an application is suspicious.

Catching edited and AI-generated documents
The problem to solve
Fake or edited payslips and bank statements can look perfect to the human eye. Build an AI tool that checks document files for signs of editing (font changes, file metadata, pixel-level anomalies), tests the figures for internal consistency, checks that employers and banks exist, and cross-checks with open-banking data where available.

Complaints handled right
Catching complaints wherever they are made
The problem to solve
Rules in many countries treat any expression of dissatisfaction as a complaint, but agents often miss them or deal with them informally. Build an AI model that spots complaints in calls, chats, emails and social media, classifies them, checks they have been logged correctly and starts the right process and deadlines.

Fixing root causes and paying redress consistently
The problem to solve
Similar complaints are often decided differently by different handlers, and the underlying causes are rarely fixed. Build an AI tool that groups complaints by real root cause, links them to the process or system at fault, recommends consistent outcomes and compensation based on past decisions and ombudsman rulings, and tracks whether fixes reduce new complaints.

Credit papers in hours, not weeks
Drafting the credit memo
The problem to solve
Analysts gather financial statements, management accounts, industry reports, news and internal data, then write long credit memos largely by hand. Build an AI agent that collects this information, drafts the memo in the bank's standard format with every figure linked to its source, highlights the key risks and mitigations, and lets the analyst edit and challenge it.

Annual reviews that refresh themselves
The problem to solve
Every existing loan must be reviewed at least once a year, and most reviews involve collecting and rewriting the same information again. Build an AI tool that updates the previous review with new financials, account behaviour, covenant results and news, shows clearly what has changed and why it matters, and recommends whether a full review is needed.

An app assistant that gets things done
Doing tasks, not just answering questions
The problem to solve
Customers want to say 'pay my rent on the 1st of every month' or 'freeze my card and send a new one' and have it done. Build an AI agent inside the app that understands the request, prepares the action, shows a clear summary for the customer to confirm, applies the right security checks and keeps a full record of what it did and why.

Answering 'why' questions about my money
The problem to solve
Customers often ask questions such as 'why is my balance lower than I expected?' or 'why was I charged this?', which normally need a call-centre agent to piece together. Build an AI assistant that looks at the customer's own transactions, pending payments, fees and holds, and gives a short, accurate explanation with links to the items involved.

Help for agents during live calls
Real-time guidance on the call
The problem to solve
Agents put customers on hold to look up procedures, and different agents give different answers to the same question. Build an AI assistant that listens to the call (with consent), understands what the customer needs, brings up the right procedure and customer details, suggests next steps and reminds the agent of required disclosures at the right moment.

Notes and follow-ups that write themselves
The problem to solve
After each call, agents spend several minutes summarising the conversation and creating follow-up tasks, and notes are often incomplete. Build an AI tool that drafts an accurate call summary, records what was agreed, creates the follow-up tasks in the right systems and lets the agent check and confirm everything in seconds.

Keeping critical services running
Spotting risky changes before they go live
The problem to solve
Large banks make thousands of technology changes every month, and a small number cause major outages, yet approvers have little time to judge risk. Build an AI model that learns from past changes and incidents, scores the risk of each new change, explains why (for example, it touches a payment system on payday) and suggests safer timing or extra testing.

An incident commander's assistant
The problem to solve
During an outage, engineers sift through alerts, logs and chat messages while managers keep asking which customers are affected and what to tell them. Build an AI agent that pulls together the timeline, links symptoms to recent changes, suggests likely causes, estimates how many customers and payments are affected, and drafts updates for customers and internal teams.

Stopping fraudsters who phone the bank
Detecting social-engineering calls
The problem to solve
Fraudsters follow scripts, call repeatedly to probe defences and use pressure tactics, but each agent sees only one call. Build an AI model that analyses call content, behaviour and patterns across many calls (for example, the same voice or phone number used on several accounts) and warns the agent in real time when a call looks like an account-takeover attempt.

Knowing it's the customer without 20 questions
The problem to solve
Genuine customers spend minutes answering security questions, which annoys them and costs agent time. Build an AI system that quietly verifies callers using signals such as the device and number used, voice, behaviour and recent app activity, with strong protection against spoofing, and asks extra questions only when the risk is high.

Disputes and chargebacks
Getting the dispute right the first time
The problem to solve
Customers raise disputes through the app or by phone, often unsure what went wrong, and staff must choose the right scheme reason code and collect the evidence. Build an AI agent that talks with the customer, works out what happened, checks whether it can be fixed without a dispute (for example, a refund that is already on its way), selects the right reason and gathers the evidence needed.

Fighting 'friendly fraud' with evidence
The problem to solve
Some cardholders claim they did not make purchases they actually made. Newer scheme rules (for example, Visa's Compelling Evidence 3.0) let merchants show proof such as matching devices and addresses from past undisputed orders, but gathering it is manual. Build an AI tool that assembles this evidence automatically and predicts which disputes are worth fighting.

Guarding the bank's own payment systems
Spotting fraudulent messages from inside the bank
The problem to solve
When criminals take over an operator's login or terminal, the payment messages they send look technically valid. Build an AI model that learns normal patterns for each operator, beneficiary and payment route, and flags unusual payment messages (new beneficiaries, odd timing, unusual amounts or sequences) for urgent checking before they are released.

Checking that only authorised payments went out
The problem to solve
Banks reconcile payments at the end of the day, but attackers can hide fraudulent messages by deleting records or altering reports, as happened in the Bangladesh case. Build an AI tool that independently compares the messages actually sent with authorised instructions and network confirmations in near real time, and raises an alert on any mismatch.

Borrowers in financial difficulty
Choosing the right kind of help
The problem to solve
Lenders can offer payment holidays, reduced payments, longer terms or a switch to interest-only, but the choice is often made quickly without knowing which option will really help. Build an AI tool that uses the customer's circumstances and the outcomes of similar past cases to recommend the support most likely to help them recover, and explains the trade-offs in plain language.

Collections with dignity
The problem to solve
Standard collections send the same letters and calls to everyone, which can feel aggressive and lead customers to ignore all contact. Build an AI system that chooses the tone, timing and channel of contact for each customer, offers self-service options to agree a plan without speaking to anyone, and checks every message for fairness and required wording.

Adviser meetings that matter
Meeting prep in minutes
The problem to solve
Before a meeting, advisers pull together portfolio performance, recent transactions, life events, open actions and relevant market news from many systems. Build an AI agent that prepares a one-page briefing for each meeting, with suggested discussion points and questions tailored to the client's goals.

From advice to suitability report
The problem to solve
When advice is given, advisers must write a detailed suitability report explaining why each recommendation fits the client's goals, attitude to risk and circumstances, which can take hours per client. Build an AI tool that drafts the report from the client's fact-find, risk profile, holdings, agreed meeting notes and the adviser's recommendation, checks that every required element is covered and flags reasoning that does not hold up.

Charging customers correctly
Matching the small print to the system
The problem to solve
Product terms, price lists and rate-change notices say one thing, but the parameters set up in the core system may say another (for example, a fee waiver that was never switched on). Build an AI agent that reads the customer documents, compares them with the system configuration and the real charges on sample accounts, and lists every mismatch with evidence for a product owner to fix.

Putting it right at scale
The problem to solve
When a charging error is found, the bank must work out who was affected, recalculate years of interest and fees, add compensation and trace customers who have moved or closed their accounts. This is usually done by large teams with spreadsheets. Build an AI-assisted remediation engine that identifies affected accounts, recalculates the correct amounts, explains each calculation and finds current contact details for former customers.

Insurance
7 themes · 20 use cases
Smarter claims handling
Fast-track for small, simple claims
The problem to solve
Thousands of small claims (for example, food spoiled in a power cut or a cracked windscreen) are handled by people one at a time and take days. Build a team of AI agents that checks the customer is covered, confirms the event really happened (for example, from weather or power-outage records), looks for warning signs, works out the amount to pay and hands a ready-to-approve recommendation to a staff member.

Reporting a claim, made easy
The problem to solve
When something goes wrong, customers have to call, wait and answer many questions, and information often arrives incomplete, leading to back-and-forth. Build an AI agent that lets customers report a claim by chat, phone or email, reads their photos and documents, checks their policy is active, captures everything needed the first time and sends it to the right team.

Check before we pay
The problem to solve
Insurers sometimes pay too much (duplicate payments, amounts above the policy limit, or forgetting the part the customer should pay themselves) or pay claims that look suspicious. Build an AI agent that reviews each claim before payment, flags anything wrong or suspicious, explains why and passes it to a person to decide.

Faster, better risk decisions
Sorting incoming requests for quotes
The problem to solve
Brokers send requests for quotes as emails with many attachments in different formats. Staff read each one to understand it, spot what is missing and decide whether the insurer even wants this kind of business. Build an AI agent that reads these packs, pulls out the key facts, flags gaps, checks the fit with what the insurer wants to cover and ranks the queue so the best opportunities are handled first.

Risk research assistant
The problem to solve
To judge a risk, staff search many sources by hand: the location, flood or wildfire exposure, the company's background, sanctions lists. Build an AI agent that gathers this from public sources and writes a short risk summary with references, highlighting any concerns.

Medical-file summary for life insurance
The problem to solve
For life and health cover, staff read long medical reports and application forms to decide whether to accept someone and on what terms. Build an AI agent that summarises the medical evidence, compares it with the insurer's rules and suggests a decision with clear reasons for a specialist to confirm.

Selling to and serving customers
Policy changes done instantly
The problem to solve
Customers often need to change their policy during the year, for example to add a driver, change a car or move house. Build an AI agent that understands the request in plain language, checks it is allowed, works out any change in price, gets the customer's agreement and updates the policy.

Sales assistant for advisors
The problem to solve
Advisors and brokers spend a lot of time comparing a customer's current cover with the products available and preparing proposals. Build an AI agent that spots gaps in a customer's cover, suggests suitable products and drafts a clear proposal.

Keeping customers when policies renew
The problem to solve
Most policies renew every year, and customers who are unhappy or find a cheaper price quietly leave. Build an AI agent that spots customers likely to leave, explains why and prepares a personalised offer or message for a staff member to approve.

Running the insurer better: operations, regulation and old systems
Understanding old systems before replacing them
The problem to solve
Insurers' core systems are often written in old programming languages, and the business rules (for example, how prices or payments are calculated) are buried in the code with little documentation. Build an AI agent that reads old code, explains the business rules in plain language and creates tests to check that a new system behaves the same way.

Staying on top of new rules
The problem to solve
Insurers must follow many regulations that change often, including new rules on how AI may be used. Build an AI agent that tracks regulatory updates, explains what changed, identifies which products, processes or AI tools are affected and drafts an action list for the compliance team to approve.

Matching the money with partners
The problem to solve
Insurers work with partner firms that sell policies and handle claims on their behalf. These partners send monthly spreadsheets in many different formats, which staff clean, combine and check against the insurer's own accounts by hand. Build an AI agent that reads these files, puts them into one standard format, matches the figures and explains any differences.

P&C
Fighting costly lawsuits in injury claims
Spotting lawsuit risk in the first hours
The problem to solve
The signs that a claim may turn into a lawsuit, such as a lawyer's letter, a delay in treatment or a referral to a particular clinic, usually appear early but are missed among hundreds of open claims. Build a team of AI agents that reads each new injury claim as soon as it is reported, scores the chance of a lawyer or lawsuit, explains the reasons and recommends the next step, such as an early call to the injured person or a fair early offer, for the adjuster to approve.

Never missing a legal deadline
The problem to solve
Lawyers often send letters demanding payment within a fixed time, sometimes at or near the policy limit. These letters arrive by post, email or as attachments and can sit unread, or their deadline can be misread. Build an AI agent that reads all incoming legal letters, picks out demands and deadlines, checks them against the policy, sets reminders and drafts a response for the adjuster and legal team to approve.

Checking medical bills and referral patterns
The problem to solve
In some injury claims, treatment is increased to raise the value of the claim, and claimants are sent to particular clinics by their lawyers. Spotting this needs someone to read every bill and compare it across many claims, which rarely happens. Build an AI agent that reviews medical bills and records, compares the treatment with what is usual for the injury, spots links between law firms, clinics and claimants across many claims, and flags concerns, with reasons, for the claims or fraud team to review.

P&C
Getting back money that others should pay
Finding who else should pay
The problem to solve
The signs that someone else caused a loss are often buried in claim notes, photos, police or fire reports and repair estimates. Adjusters may not notice them, or notice them too late. Build an AI agent that reviews every open and recently paid claim, spots who else could be responsible, estimates how much could be recovered and how likely it is to succeed, and sends the best opportunities to the recovery team.

Building and chasing the recovery case
The problem to solve
Once an opportunity is found, staff must gather evidence, work out the amount, write to the responsible party or their insurer and follow up, often before a legal time limit runs out. For complex losses such as a battery fire, several parties may share the blame. Build a team of AI agents that collects the evidence, works out who owes what, drafts the demand letters or claim forms, tracks time limits and replies, and keeps staff updated on each case.

Claiming what reinsurers owe
The problem to solve
Insurers pass part of their large or catastrophe claims to reinsurers. Working out what each reinsurer owes means applying complex contract terms to every claim, and claims that qualify can be missed or reported late, especially after a large storm. Build an AI agent that reads the reinsurance contracts, checks each claim against them, works out the amount owed, prepares the claim to the reinsurer and tracks payment.

P&C
Seeing hidden long-term liabilities before they become losses
Early warning for emerging risks
The problem to solve
Early clues to a new liability appear in small ways: a few similar claims, a new scientific study, a regulator's action, a first lawsuit. No single person sees them all. Build a team of AI agents that continuously scans the insurer's own claims and policy data, along with outside sources such as research, regulator actions, news and court filings, links the signals together and raises explained warnings for actuaries and underwriters to accept or reject.

Measuring how exposed the business is
The problem to solve
When a new risk is flagged, the insurer needs to know which customers, policies and years could be affected, whether the policy wording covers it and how much reinsurance would respond. Today this means weeks of manual searching through old policies and claims. Build an AI agent that finds the affected customers and policies, reads the wording to see whether the risk is covered or excluded, estimates the possible cost and suggests actions, such as new exclusions, price changes or extra reinsurance, for leaders to decide.

Capital Markets
6 themes · 12 use cases
Protecting deal secrets and market surveillance
Guarding deal secrets across the wall
The problem to solve
When bankers start work on a merger or a bond issue, the names go on a watch or restricted list, and bankers sometimes 'wall-cross' investors to sound them out. Checking that nobody on the trading side touched those names, or the deal's likely targets and peers, is mostly manual. Build an agent network that reads deal-team mandates, keeps the watch and restricted lists up to date, looks for trading, research or chat activity in related names, and drafts an evidence pack for Compliance, with every finding linked to its source record and access limited by role so deal details never reach the wrong team.

Triage of surveillance alerts that explains itself
The problem to solve
Surveillance systems flag possible spoofing, layering, front-running or marking-the-close, but over 90% of alerts are false and each takes an analyst hours to clear. Build an AI investigator that pulls the order book, the trader's history, related chats and calls, and market news around the event, decides whether it is likely genuine, writes a plain-language case note and recommends close, escalate or monitor. It learns from past closed cases and the market-abuse rules to keep decisions consistent, and an analyst signs off every closure.

Settlement and post-trade operations
Predicting and preventing settlement fails
The problem to solve
Trades fail because of wrong settlement instructions, missing securities, late funding, mismatched details or a counterparty that is slow to confirm. Teams usually find out on the morning of settlement, which is too late. Build an agent network that scores every open trade for fail risk using counterparty history, security availability, instruction status and cut-off times, and recommends a fix such as chasing an instruction, borrowing the stock, using partial settlement or re-routing.

Breaks and confirmations that resolve themselves
The problem to solve
Confirmations and counterparty statements arrive as PDFs, emails and SWIFT messages in different layouts, and each mismatch (price, quantity, date, account) is solved by hand. Build an AI agent that reads incoming confirmations, matches them to the firm's own record, finds the cause of each break, drafts the message to the counterparty and, where rules allow, corrects the booking after approval.

Liquidity, cash and collateral
Intraday liquidity and cash-positioning agent
The problem to solve
Treasury must predict how much cash will leave and arrive across accounts, currencies and payment systems during the day, and decide when to move funds, borrow or sell assets. Build an agent network that combines payment queues, nostro balances, expected settlements and market-hours data, predicts the cash position by hour, warns before a shortfall and suggests the cheapest way to cover it.

Turning legal agreements into live rules
The problem to solve
Terms in ISDA master agreements and Credit Support Annexes (thresholds, eligible collateral, haircuts, notice times) are in long PDFs, and teams re-key them into systems by hand. Build an AI agent that reads each agreement, extracts the key terms with page references, compares them to what is set up in the collateral system, flags mismatches and drafts the correction request, with every extracted term linked to its clause so legal can check it in seconds.

Trade capture and regulatory reporting
A trading assistant that checks as it captures
The problem to solve
Sales traders and dealers type or say trade details into the order and trade management system, and a slip in quantity, currency or counterparty may not be caught until much later. Build an AI assistant that takes a request in plain words or from a chat, fills in the trade ticket, checks it against client limits, mandate and best-execution rules, and shows a clear summary for the trader to confirm before booking.

Fixing regulatory trade reports before they bounce
The problem to solve
Trade reports for EMIR Refit, MiFIR, SFTR and similar regimes are rejected by trade repositories for missing or wrong data, such as legal entity IDs, product codes or timestamps, and fixing them is manual. Build an AI agent network that checks each report against the rules before submission, explains why rejected reports failed, traces the error back to its source system and suggests the fix with evidence.

Market data, valuations and P&L
Catching bad market data before it hits valuations
The problem to solve
Stale curves, bad ticks and missing volatility points quietly corrupt end-of-day P&L and risk. Build an agent network that watches every price feed, flags anomalies, tests them against related instruments and proposes a repair, such as an interpolated value or a fallback source, before the end-of-day cut-off.

Explaining the day's P&L in minutes
The problem to solve
Every morning, desks need to know why P&L moved and how much is unexplained. Build an agent that calls the risk engine, splits the move into rates, FX, volatility, carry, new trades and cancellations, and chases any leftover amount to a likely cause such as a missing trade, a late price or a wrong rate.

Execution quality and market resilience
Picking the right execution algo for each order
The problem to solve
Traders choose algos (VWAP, TWAP, implementation shortfall, dark-pool-first) by habit. Build an agent network that reads the order's size, liquidity, volatility and urgency, predicts the cost of each strategy and recommends one. A second agent scores the result against the benchmark and feeds the outcome back so the choice improves over time.

A simulated market to stress-test trading controls
The problem to solve
Risk limits, kill switches and trading controls are tested on a few past events. Build a simulated market where AI agents behave as different participants (market makers, funds, panicked sellers), replay a flash crash or a liquidity freeze, and show where the firm's controls fail or react too slowly.

